          "post_id": "494347",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following context-free grammar $G$, with the set of variables $\\{S, A, C, X, Y\\}$, the set of terminal symbols $\\{a, b, c\\}$, and $S$ as the start variable. The production rules are:</p><p>$$<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>& S \\rightarrow X C \\mid A Y \\\\<br>& X \\rightarrow a X b \\mid a b \\\\<br>& A \\rightarrow a A \\mid a \\\\<br>& Y \\rightarrow b Y c \\mid b c \\\\<br>& C \\rightarrow c C \\mid c<br>\\end{aligned}<br>$$</p><p><br>Which ONE of the following statements correctly describes the language $L(G)$ generated by this grammar?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$L(G)=\\left\\{a^i b^j c^k \\mid i, j, k \\geq 1\\right.$ and $(i=j$ and $\\left.j=k)\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L(G)=\\left\\{a^i b^j c^k \\mid i, j, k \\geq 1\\right.$ and $(i=k$ or $\\left.i=j)\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L(G)=\\left\\{a^i b^j c^k \\mid i, j, k \\geq 1\\right.$ and $(i=j$ or $\\left.j=k)\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L(G)=\\left\\{a^i b^j c^k \\mid i, j, k \\geq 1\\right.$ and $\\left.i=j+k\\right\\}$</li></ol>",
